import Mathlib
import Shared.CarmichaelHelper
import Shared.CarmichaelProof

/-! # The complete local-to-global obstruction for Fibonacci primitive divisors

`Shared.CarmichaelProof` introduces the **computable primitive part** `primPart n`
of `F n`: starting from `F n`, it strips away every prime factor shared with `F d`
for each proper divisor `d ∣ n`.  That file proves *one* direction of the
fundamental criterion,

> `1 < primPart n  →  F n` has a primitive prime divisor
> (`primPart_implies_primitive`),

which is exactly what powers the `native_decide` verification for `n ≤ 10000`.

This file supplies the **converse**, and hence the full biconditional:

> **`1 < primPart n  ↔  F n` has a primitive prime divisor**
> (`one_lt_primPart_iff_hasPrimitive`).

Conceptually this is a *local-to-global / obstruction* statement.  The proper
divisors `d ∣ n` carry the **local data** (which primes have already appeared by
index `d`); `primPart n` is the **obstruction class** measuring what survives the
gluing.  The biconditional says the obstruction is *sharp*: it is nontrivial
(`> 1`) **iff** a genuinely new (primitive) prime appears at the global index `n`.
The forward direction (gluing exists ⇒ obstruction vanishes-to-`1`'s failure) was
known; the converse (a primitive prime *forces* the obstruction to be nontrivial)
is the content here, completing the picture begun in Bridge-style entry-point
theory (`Applications.RankOfApparition.fibRank_dvd_iff`,
`Speculative.AutoResearch.CarmichaelComposite.primitive_of_fibCoprimePart_pos`).

## Main results

* `stripAllAux_keeps_coprime_factor` — a prime coprime to the stripping modulus is
  never removed.
* `primPart_keeps_primitive` — a primitive prime divisor of `F n` survives into
  `primPart n`.
* `hasPrimitive_imp_one_lt_primPart` — the converse direction.
* `one_lt_primPart_iff_hasPrimitive` — **the complete obstruction biconditional.**

-- !-- Lab Notebook -- !--
Hypothesis : The computable obstruction `primPart n` detects primitive prime
             divisors *exactly*, not just sufficiently.
Result     : Confirmed, `sorry = 0`.  A primitive prime `p` (entry point exactly
             `n`) is coprime to `F d` for every proper divisor `d ∣ n`, so each
             `stripAllAux` step — which only divides out a `gcd` with `F d` — leaves
             `v_p` untouched; hence `p ∣ primPart n` and `primPart n > 1`.  Combined
             with `primPart_implies_primitive` this gives the biconditional.
Insight    : The repeated-gcd "strip" is a *p-adically inert* operation away from
             the stripped modulus: coprimality is preserved fibrewise, which is the
             algebraic reason the local obstruction is faithful.  Primitivity is thus
             equivalent to a single decidable arithmetic predicate, which is why the
             `native_decide` census is not merely sufficient but *characteristic*.
Failure    : The biconditional reduces Carmichael's theorem to "`primPart n > 1` for
             all composite `n`"; the infinite composite tail still needs the
             cyclotomic size estimate (the deep `sorry` in `Shared.CarmichaelProof`),
             which Mathlib lacks and which is the standing open frontier.
-- !-- End Lab Notebook -- !--
-/

set_option maxHeartbeats 800000

open Nat

/-- `F n` has a **primitive prime divisor**: a prime dividing `F n` but no earlier
positive-index Fibonacci number. -/
def HasPrimitivePrimeDivisor (n : ℕ) : Prop :=
  ∃ p, Nat.Prime p ∧ p ∣ Nat.fib n ∧ ∀ k, 0 < k → k < n → ¬(p ∣ Nat.fib k)

/-
!-- A prime `p` dividing `r` but coprime to `m` is never stripped: each step
!-- divides `r` by `gcd r m`, which is coprime to `p`, so `p` survives. -- !--

**Stripping preserves a coprime prime factor.**  If a prime `p` divides `r` but
does not divide the stripping modulus `m`, then `p` still divides `stripAllAux r m fuel`.
-/
theorem stripAllAux_keeps_coprime_factor (p : ℕ) (hp : Nat.Prime p) (m : ℕ)
    (hpm : ¬ p ∣ m) : ∀ (fuel r : ℕ), p ∣ r → p ∣ stripAllAux r m fuel := by
  intro fuel r hr; induction' fuel with fuel ih generalizing r <;> simp_all +decide [ stripAllAux ] ;
  split_ifs <;> simp_all +decide [ Nat.dvd_gcd_iff ];
  refine' ih _ _;
  refine' Nat.dvd_div_of_mul_dvd _;
  exact Nat.Coprime.mul_dvd_of_dvd_of_dvd ( Nat.Coprime.coprime_dvd_left ( Nat.gcd_dvd_right _ _ ) ( Nat.Coprime.symm <| hp.coprime_iff_not_dvd.mpr hpm ) ) ( Nat.gcd_dvd_left _ _ ) hr

/-
!-- Fold the previous lemma over the proper-divisor list: every `F d` in the fold
!-- is coprime to a primitive `p` (entry point `n`), so `p` survives the whole fold. -- !--

**A primitive prime divisor survives into the primitive part.**  If `p` is a
prime dividing `F n` with `p ∤ F k` for all `0 < k < n`, then `p ∣ primPart n`.
-/
theorem primPart_keeps_primitive (n p : ℕ) (hp : Nat.Prime p)
    (hpn : p ∣ Nat.fib n) (hprim : ∀ k, 0 < k → k < n → ¬ p ∣ Nat.fib k) :
    p ∣ primPart n := by
  -- By induction on the list, we can show that each step of the fold preserves the divisibility by p.
  have h_ind : ∀ (L : List ℕ), (∀ d ∈ L, ¬(p ∣ Nat.fib d)) → ∀ (acc : ℕ), p ∣ acc → p ∣ List.foldl (fun r d => stripAllAux r (Nat.fib d) r) acc L := by
    intro L hL acc hacc; induction' L using List.reverseRecOn with d L ih <;> simp_all +decide [ Nat.dvd_add_right ] ;
    exact stripAllAux_keeps_coprime_factor p hp ( fib L ) ( hL L ( Or.inr rfl ) ) _ _ ih;
  apply h_ind;
  · unfold propDivs; aesop;
  · assumption

/-
!-- `p ∣ primPart n` and `primPart n ∣ F n > 0` give `2 ≤ p ≤ primPart n`. -- !--

**Converse obstruction direction.**  If `F n` has a primitive prime divisor and
`0 < n`, then the primitive part exceeds `1`.
-/
theorem hasPrimitive_imp_one_lt_primPart (n : ℕ) (hn : 0 < n)
    (h : HasPrimitivePrimeDivisor n) : 1 < primPart n := by
  obtain ⟨ p, hp₁, hp₂, hp₃ ⟩ := h;
  have h_pos : 0 < primPart n := by
    exact Nat.pos_of_dvd_of_pos ( primPart_dvd n ) ( Nat.fib_pos.mpr hn );
  exact lt_of_lt_of_le hp₁.one_lt ( Nat.le_of_dvd h_pos ( primPart_keeps_primitive n p hp₁ hp₂ hp₃ ) )

-- !-- `↔`: `→` is the catalog's `primPart_implies_primitive`; `←` is the converse above. -- !--
/-- **The complete local-to-global obstruction biconditional.**  For `n ≥ 3`, the
computable primitive part `primPart n` exceeds `1` **iff** `F n` has a primitive
prime divisor.  The obstruction is therefore an exact, decidable characterization
of primitivity — completing the one-directional criterion of
`Shared.CarmichaelProof.primPart_implies_primitive`. -/
theorem one_lt_primPart_iff_hasPrimitive (n : ℕ) (hn : 3 ≤ n) :
    1 < primPart n ↔ HasPrimitivePrimeDivisor n := by
  constructor
  · intro h
    exact primPart_implies_primitive n hn h
  · intro h
    exact hasPrimitive_imp_one_lt_primPart n (by omega) h